# MCP Agent Bus — inter-session coordination

This project uses a local, event-driven message bus (the `mcp-agent-bus` MCP
server). Sessions talk to each other through it: any session can send a prompt
to another session, which runs it and replies to the sender.

## Your session identity
- Your session name comes from the `AGENT_SESSION_NAME` environment variable.
  Run `echo "$AGENT_SESSION_NAME"` once at the start of a session to read it.
- If `AGENT_SESSION_NAME` is empty, ask the user once: "What should this
  session be called on the agent bus?" Use a short lowercase handle
  (e.g. `backend`, `frontend`, `reviewer`). Remember it for the whole session.
- Never guess or reuse another session's name.

## Two ways to receive work

### A. On-demand (interactive session)
An interactive agent session cannot reliably auto-wake from background output,
so it receives work only when it is active. To pick up messages, call
`bus_receive(me="<yourname>", block=true, timeout_ms=45000)` (keep the timeout
under ~50s; MCP clients may abort longer blocking calls). Then run each
message's `text` as a task and reply via `bus_send`. Re-run `bus_receive` to
keep waiting.

### B. Autonomous (headless worker) — recommended for a standing worker
For hands-free processing, run the headless worker in a plain terminal pane
(not inside an agent session):

```
MCP_AGENT_BUS_DIR="$PWD/bus" \
WORKER_CWD="$PWD" \
node src/worker.mjs <yourname> --model <your-model>
```

It blocks on the inbox, runs each incoming task with `cursor-agent -p`
(headless), and replies to the sender automatically.

## Handling a received task (either mode)
1. Parse the message. Fields: `from`, `to`, `subject`, `text`, `id`, `ts`.
2. Treat `text` as a task/prompt from the `from` session; do it with normal tools.
3. Reply: `bus_send(to=<message.from>, from="<yourname>", subject="re: <subject>", text=<concise result>)`.

## Sending / coordinating (bus_* MCP tools)
- `bus_send(to, from, text, subject?)` — send a task/message to another session.
- `bus_receive(me, block?, timeout_ms?)` — fetch + consume your messages
  (blocks until one arrives when `block=true`).
- `bus_broadcast(from, text)` / `bus_read_broadcasts(me)` — global announcements.
- `bus_peek(me)` — look at your inbox without consuming.
- `bus_list_sessions()` — see which sessions have inboxes.

Always set `from`/`me` to your own session name.

## Guardrails
- Only ONE receiver per session name (headless worker OR interactive receive —
  never both draining the same inbox).
- A headless worker runs tasks with `cursor-agent -p ... --force` (auto-approves
  shell/write). Only run it for trusted senders. Keep replies concise and avoid
  destructive actions (deletes, force-push, production changes) unless explicit
  and clearly safe; otherwise reply asking for confirmation.
- To stop a headless worker: Ctrl-C its terminal (or kill the `worker.mjs` PID).
- Disable this behavior entirely by removing this rule file.
